BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (UFS2 was developed for FreeBSD during the transition to multi-terabyte storage. Marshall) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (Kirk McKusick's BSDCon 2003 paper describes the reasons for a new on-disk format rather) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (than indefinite enlargement of UFS1. It retained the BSD cylinder-group and Fast File) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (System philosophy while changing structures that were becoming too small or too) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (restrictive.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (The design introduced wider block pointers, higher-resolution timestamps, extended) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (attributes and a larger boot block area, with modifications to soft updates. It built on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (decades of BSD storage design rather than attempting to be a ZFS-style pooled copy-on-write) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (filesystem. UFS1 remained an important compatibility format, but UFS2 should be identified) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (separately.) Tj ET
